<review_exercises chapter="6">
  <exercise number="253" has_solution="true">
    <problem>
      <p>If the radius of convergence for a power series $$\sum\limits_{n = 0}^{\infty}{a_{n}x^{n}}$$ is $$5,$$ then the radius of convergence for the series $$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}{na_{n}x^{n - 1}}$$ is also $$5.$$</p>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <p>True</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise number="254" has_solution="false">
    <problem>
      <p>Power series can be used to show that the derivative of $$e^{x}\ \text{is}\ e^{x}.$$ (Hint: Recall that $$e^{x} = {\sum\limits_{n = 0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n\text{!}}}x^{n}.)$$</p>
    </problem>
  </exercise>
  <exercise number="255" has_solution="true">
    <problem>
      <p>For small values of $$x,{\text{sin}\mspace{2mu} x \approx x.}$$</p>
